    /// between identical requests, as they do for a person.
    ///
    /// `q`: The question to ask Google AI Mode, e.g. 'how to learn python fast'.
    ///
    /// Options are setters on the returned [`GoogleAiMode`].
    ///
    /// Calls `POST /search/google-ai-mode`.
    pub fn google_ai_mode(&self, q: impl Into<String>) -> GoogleAiMode {
        GoogleAiMode::new(self.clone(), q.into())
    }

    /// Search Google Lens with an image URL.
    ///
    /// Runs a Google Lens search for the image at `url` and returns its visual matches as JSON
    /// (usually ~60). Every match carries `title`, `url` (the page the image is on), `domain`,
    /// `source` (site name), `imageUrl` (the matching image), `thumbnailUrl`, `width`, `height` and
    /// `position`; product matches add `price` and `currency` (and `product` details) when Google
    /// has them, and `priceText`, `rating` and `reviews` as the tile shows them. `relatedSearches`
    /// lists what Lens recognised in the image (e.g. 'Eiffel Tower', with its Knowledge Graph id)
    /// and `aiOverview` is Google's own description of the image when it is ready in time (else
    /// null). Shopping matches follow the country the request comes from - set `proxy_country` for
    /// a market (a US request is served from a European exit, where Google loads Lens matches;
    /// `proxyCountry` in the response says which).
    ///
    /// `url`: Public http(s) URL of the image to search with, e.g. a product photo. Google fetches
    /// the image itself.
    ///
    /// Options are setters on the returned [`GoogleLens`].
    ///
    /// Calls `POST /images/google-lens`.
    pub fn google_lens(&self, url: impl Into<String>) -> GoogleLens {
        GoogleLens::new(self.clone(), url.into())
    }

    /// Ask Google Gemini and get its answer and cited sources.
    ///
    /// Asks Google Gemini (gemini.google.com, signed out) one question and returns `answer` (plain
    /// text) and `answerMarkdown`, `citations` (pages cited inline: `position`, `title`, `url`,
    /// `domain`, `siteName`, `description`, `citedText` - the answer sentences that page backs -
    /// and `firstCitedAt`, the character offset of the first of them), `sources` (pages Gemini
    /// consulted when it searched the web: `position`, `title`, `url`, `domain`), `webSearch`
    /// (whether it searched the web), `followUpPrompts` (`label`, `prompt`), `richBlocks` (cards
    /// Gemini drew that have no text, e.g. a sports score or image strip, left out of the answer),
    /// `model` (the model label the app shows), `location` and `locationCountry` (where Gemini
    /// placed the visitor), `complete` and `conversationId`. `country` asks from that country.
    /// Answers vary between identical calls, as they do for people. One unit per call.
    ///
    /// `prompt`: The question to ask Gemini, as a user would type it, e.g. 'best CRM for small
    /// business' (max 4000 characters). Each call starts a new conversation.
    ///
    /// Options are setters on the returned [`Gemini`].
    ///
    /// Calls `POST /ai/gemini`.
    pub fn gemini(&self, prompt: impl Into<String>) -> Gemini {
        Gemini::new(self.clone(), prompt.into())
    }

    /// Ask ChatGPT and get its answer and cited sites.
    ///
    /// Asks ChatGPT (chatgpt.com, signed out) one question and returns its finished answer:
    /// `answer` (plain text) and `answerMarkdown`, `citations` (the source pills ChatGPT attaches
    /// to claims: `position`, `siteName`, `domain`, `moreSources` - how many further sources the
    /// pill groups - and `citedText`, the answer text it backs), `links` (links written into the
    /// answer: `position`, `title`, `url`, `domain`), `sources` (every site cited or linked, once),
    /// `webSearch` (whether ChatGPT searched the web), `model` (null: signed-out ChatGPT does not
    /// show it) and `conversationId`. Signed-out ChatGPT shows the full URL of a cited page only on
    /// hover, so citations carry the site and domain, not the page URL. `country` asks from that
    /// country. Answers take 15-60 s and vary between identical calls, as they do for people. One
    /// unit per call; a 503 when ChatGPT capacity is busy is not billed.
    ///
    /// `prompt`: The question to ask ChatGPT, as a user would type it, e.g. 'best CRM for small
    /// business' (max 2000 characters). Each call starts a new conversation.
    ///
    /// Options are setters on the returned [`Chatgpt`].
